        "comment": "Static archive and payload decoding, 2026-10-25 UTC. SHA-256 verified; 4,468,167-byte gzip/tar community sample. The archive has 24 entries (20 regular files and four directories). No member path was extracted to the host filesystem. Member .rsync/b/run (SHA-256 9c1a0d7983d58ada34d17bf1413946cee98fbf1ce67ea2f60d8b237e1f9f3cb9) contains three base64-encoded Perl IRC bots: ea38705428b80ac1970ce163e9b288f648da9d5766d74117374a61799f07581c (27,803 bytes), bc111a15b6036a8a9e78a466b27abbb0a20d24cae4ed5948f3b947e43632de79 (27,806 bytes), and c0af7d5ebcdc0f03886c4286fcb2c0409c4a30f706529f9b3d1aeb2f4274a146 (27,806 bytes). Their server literals are 0xB32B8B53, 179.43.139.83 and 185.196.8.171; the hexadecimal literal is the same IPv4 address as the second bot, so these represent two unique endpoints, 179.43.139.83:443 and 185.196.8.171:443. Decoded line 224 passes the configured server and port to conectar; line 204 creates IO::Socket::INET with Proto tcp and subsequent code emits IRC USER/NICK messages. Port 443 here is plain IRC over TCP, not evidence of TLS. Decoded scripts include shell-command and network-attack handlers. Other package scripts launch kswapd00, replace cron entries, interfere with competing processes and modify SSH authorized_keys; key material is omitted. Miner and scanner components are contextual package members, not automatically separate malware submissions. The first endpoint already exists in ThreatFox as PerlBot/ShellBot IOC 1815637 and was not resubmitted. Archive first_seen is 2026-10-25 03:51:08 UTC; this supports a recent sample observation, not a verified current bot session. No script, Perl payload, ELF, scanner or C2 connection was executed."
